      "exam_answer_md": "An **isoquant** defines the combinations of inputs (K, L) that yield the same level of output; its shape shows the degree of substitutability. The **MRTS** is the rate at which two inputs are substituted while maintaining the same output: **MRTS_KL = MPL / MPK**."
    },
    {
      "id": "be-s8-isoquants-isocosts-c2",
      "heading": "Three isoquant shapes",
      "explanation_md": "- **Linear isoquants** (Q = aK + bL): capital and labour are **perfect substitutes**. MRTS_KL = **b/a**, a constant rate whatever the input levels.\n- **Leontief isoquants** (Q = min{bK, cL}): capital and labour are **perfect complements**, used in fixed proportions. The isoquants are L-shaped and there is **no substitution** along them, so no MRTS.\n- **Cobb-Douglas isoquants** (Q = K^a L^b): inputs are **not perfectly substitutable**. The MRTS **diminishes**: as you use less of one input, you need increasingly more of the other to keep output the same.",
      "example_ids": [],
      "key_terms": [],
      "exam_line": "Linear: constant MRTS = b/a. Leontief: L-shaped, no MRTS. Cobb-Douglas: convex, diminishing MRTS.",
      "clarification": null,
      "check_questions": [
