    {
      "id": "be-s8-isoquants-isocosts-c3",
      "heading": "Isocost lines",
      "explanation_md": "An **isocost line** shows the input combinations that cost the same: **wL + rK = C**, where w is the wage and r the rental rate of capital. Rearranged: **K = (1/r)C - (w/r)L**, so the intercepts are C/r on the capital axis and C/w on the labour axis, and the slope is -w/r.\n\n- Isocosts **farther from the origin** mean **higher costs** (C0 < C1).\n- A change in an input price **changes the slope**: a lower wage swings the labour intercept out from C/w0 to C/w1.",
      "example_ids": [],
      "key_terms": [
        {
          "term": "Isocost line",
          "meaning": "Input combinations with the same total cost: wL + rK = C",
          "source_ref": "be--session-8#19"
        }
      ],
      "exam_line": "Isocost: wL + rK = C, intercepts C/r and C/w, slope -w/r; a price change rotates it.",
      "clarification": null,
      "check_questions": [
